### What is the Rotate LinkedList problem?

Given the head of a singly linked list and a non-negative integer k, rotate the list to the right by k places. This means the last k nodes move to the front. For example, rotating 1->2->3->4->5 right by 2 gives 4->5->1->2->3.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RotateLinkedList {
    public ListNode rotateRight(ListNode head, int k) {
        if (head == null || head.next == null || k == 0) return head;

        // Find length and tail
        int length = 1;
        ListNode tail = head;
        while (tail.next != null) {
            tail = tail.next;
            length++;
        }

        // Reduce k
        k = k % length;
        if (k == 0) return head;

        // Form ring
        tail.next = head;

        // Walk to new tail: (length - k) steps from head
        ListNode newTail = head;
        for (int i = 1; i < length - k; i++) {
            newTail = newTail.next;
        }

        // Cut
        ListNode newHead = newTail.next;
        newTail.next = null;

        return newHead;
    }
}

class ListNode {
    int val;
    ListNode next;
    ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
}
```

### Why form a ring?

Forming a ring by connecting the tail to the head simplifies the operation. Instead of detaching and reattaching two separate segments (error-prone with pointer management), we have a single circular list and just need to find where to cut. One cut gives us the rotated list.

```java
// Without ring: need to manage 4 pointers
// prevNewHead.next = null (new tail)
// tail.next = head (reconnect)
// newHead = prevNewHead.next (save before cutting)

// With ring: form loop, find cut point, cut once
tail.next = head;  // ring
// walk to new tail
newTail.next = null; // cut
```

### Why use k % length?

Rotating by the full length returns the original list. Any k larger than the length is equivalent to k % length rotations. This also handles the case where k is 0 or a multiple of the length, where we return the original head without modification.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ModuloDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int length = 5;
        System.out.println("k=2: effective = " + (2 % length));  // 2
        System.out.println("k=7: effective = " + (7 % length));  // 2
        System.out.println("k=5: effective = " + (5 % length));  // 0 (no-op)
        System.out.println("k=0: effective = " + (0 % length));  // 0 (no-op)
    }
}
```

```text
k=2: effective = 2
k=7: effective = 2
k=5: effective = 0
k=0: effective = 0
```

### How do you find the cut point?

The new tail is at position (length - k) from the original head. We walk (length - k - 1) steps from the head to reach the new tail. The node after it becomes the new head.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class CutPointDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // List: 1->2->3->4->5, k=2, length=5
        // New tail at position: 5 - 2 = 3 (1-indexed)
        // Walk 2 steps from head: head(1)->2->3
        // New tail = node 3, new head = node 4
        // Result: 4->5->1->2->3
        System.out.println("Cut after position " + (5 - 2) + " from head");
        System.out.println("Walk " + (5 - 2 - 1) + " steps from head");
    }
}
```

```text
Cut after position 3 from head
Walk 2 steps from head
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty list, a single-node list, k equal to zero, and k being a multiple of the list length. All of these return the original head unchanged.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RotateEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RotateLinkedList solver = new RotateLinkedList();

        // Empty list
        System.out.println(solver.rotateRight(null, 3));  // null

        // Single node
        ListNode single = new ListNode(1);
        System.out.println(solver.rotateRight(single, 5).val);  // 1

        // k = 0
        ListNode list = new ListNode(1);
        list.next = new ListNode(2);
        System.out.println(solver.rotateRight(list, 0).val);  // 1
    }
}
```

```text
null
1
1
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is trying to detach and reattach two segments manually without forming a ring, leading to pointer errors. Another mistake is forgetting the modulo step, causing the algorithm to walk past the end of the list for large k values.

```java
// WRONG: no modulo, k=1000000 on a 5-node list
for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) { /* walks 1000000 steps! */ }

// CORRECT: reduce k first
k = k % length; // now at most length-1 steps
```

### How is this different from rotating an array?

Array rotation uses the reverse-three-times trick (reverse whole, reverse first k, reverse rest) because random access is available. Linked list rotation uses the ring-and-cut approach because we can only traverse sequentially.

| Aspect | Array Rotation | LinkedList Rotation |
|---|---|---|
| Access | Random (index-based) | Sequential (pointer-based) |
| Strategy | Reverse three times | Form ring, cut |
| Extra space | O(1) (in-place reversal) | O(1) (pointer manipulation) |
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |

```java
// Array: reverse-three-times
reverse(arr, 0, n - 1);
reverse(arr, 0, k - 1);
reverse(arr, k, n - 1);

// LinkedList: ring and cut
tail.next = head;
// walk to new tail, cut
```

### How would you apply this in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, a daily on-call roster of settlement staff is stored as a linked list. Each day the roster rotates right by k positions so the last k staff cycle to the front. The ring-and-cut approach rotates the roster in place with no extra memory.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class OnCallRosterService {
    public ListNode rotateRoster(ListNode roster, int daysRotation) {
        if (roster == null || roster.next == null || daysRotation == 0)
            return roster;
        int length = 1;
        ListNode tail = roster;
        while (tail.next != null) { tail = tail.next; length++; }
        daysRotation = daysRotation % length;
        if (daysRotation == 0) return roster;
        tail.next = roster;
        ListNode newTail = roster;
        for (int i = 1; i < length - daysRotation; i++)
            newTail = newTail.next;
        ListNode newHead = newTail.next;
        newTail.next = null;
        return newHead;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that forming a ring is cleaner than manual segment management. They should also recognise that rotating left by k is the same as rotating right by (length - k), so one implementation handles both directions. The ring-and-cut pattern also applies to other cyclic list problems.

